<!-- ─── §01 The Problem ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Parallelism Trap</div>
<h2>Autoregressive decoding is serial by construction</h2>
<p>Every autoregressive (AR) language model generates tokens one at a time, left to right. Token 42 cannot be predicted until tokens 1–41 are finalized. This strict sequentiality is the <em>fundamental efficiency bottleneck</em> for LLM inference — even a 1000-token response requires 1000 sequential forward passes.</p>
<p>Diffusion language models offer an alternative: instead of generating left-to-right, they start with all tokens masked and iteratively unmask them in parallel. A single forward pass can commit dozens of tokens simultaneously, giving a theoretical speedup proportional to the number of tokens unmasked per step.</p>
<p>But full-sequence diffusion — where <em>every</em> token in the response is denoised jointly — struggles with long chain-of-thought reasoning. Without a left-to-right causal spine, the model cannot reliably build on its own earlier reasoning steps, causing incoherence in extended problem-solving.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">serial vs parallel decoding — the trade-off</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover / click to explore</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-tradeoff" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px;cursor:pointer"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The key question DreamReasoner asks: <strong>can we get parallel generation without sacrificing reasoning?</strong> The answer turns out to hinge entirely on how you train — specifically on what <em>block size</em> you use.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §02 Block Diffusion ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-blockdiff" data-code="blockdiff">
<div class="section-no">02 — Block Diffusion</div>
<h2>A hybrid: autoregressive across blocks, diffusion within each block</h2>
<p>Block diffusion is an architectural compromise between pure AR and pure diffusion. The output sequence is partitioned into <strong>K contiguous blocks</strong>, each containing <strong>B tokens</strong>. Blocks are generated autoregressively (block 2 only starts after block 1 finishes), but <em>within</em> each block all B tokens are denoised in parallel using bidirectional context.</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Block diffusion factorization</div>
<span class="math-var">p</span>θ(x₀) = ∏<sub>k=1..K</sub> <span class="math-var">p</span>θ(b<sup>k</sup>₀ | b<sup>&lt;k</sup>₀)  <span class="math-comment">← autoregressive across blocks</span>
<br>
<span class="math-var">p</span>θ(b<sup>k</sup>₀ | b<sup>&lt;k</sup>₀) = diffusion denoising  <span class="math-comment">← parallel within each block</span>
<br><br>
Block size B=1 → pure autoregressive  <span class="math-comment">(1 token per block)</span>
Block size B=N → pure diffusion  <span class="math-comment">(all N tokens in one block)</span>
</div>

<p>Block size <strong>B</strong> is the key knob. Small B ≈ AR (safe, slow). Large B ≈ full diffusion (fast, risky). The efficiency metric is <strong>tokens per forward pass (TPF)</strong> — how many tokens get committed in each forward pass of the model. AR has TPF = 1. Full diffusion can have TPF = many dozens. Block diffusion sits in between, with TPF scaling with B.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">block diffusion — interactive structure</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a block size to see the structure</span>
  </div>
