<!-- ─── §03 Granularity Collapse ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-collapse" data-code="collapse">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Granularity Collapse</div>
<h2>Training with large blocks destroys reasoning ability</h2>
<p>Here is the paper's central empirical finding — a result that is stark enough to be called a <em>collapse</em>. When you train a diffusion language model with a fixed large block size (B=32), reasoning performance on hard benchmarks like AIME drops catastrophically:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">AIME 2024 accuracy — training block size vs inference block size</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">choose a training configuration</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="btn-group" id="collapse-train-btns">
      <button class="bsz-btn active" data-train="4">Trained B=4 (fine)</button>
      <button class="bsz-btn" data-train="32">Trained B=32 (coarse)</button>
      <button class="bsz-btn" data-train="curr">Curriculum 4→32</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-collapse" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div id="collapse-label" style="font-size:.73rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);text-align:center"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The contrast is dramatic. A model trained on small blocks (B=4) achieves <strong>47.1% on AIME 2024</strong> under diffusion decoding — and maintains that even when you swap inference to large blocks. But the same architecture trained with large blocks (B=32) collapses to <strong>20.0%</strong> under fine-grained inference — a 57% relative drop.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Why does large-block training cause this?</strong> When B=32, the model learns to rely on long-range intra-block attention — it sees 32 tokens simultaneously and builds dependencies that span the whole block. But at inference time with smaller blocks, those long-range dependencies are unavailable. The model has overfit to a decoding structure it can't fall back from.
</div>

<p>Conversely, small-block training builds <em>local causal structure</em>: the model learns tight sequential dependencies within 4-token windows. This knowledge transfers naturally to larger blocks — it can still generate 32 tokens in parallel, just using the robust local patterns it learned from 4-token training.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §04 Curriculum Fix ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-curriculum" data-code="curriculum">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Curriculum Fix</div>
<h2>Start fine-grained, finish coarse-grained</h2>
<p>The fix is conceptually simple: instead of training at a fixed large block size, train in a <em>curriculum</em> that starts at B=4 (building strong local causal reasoning) and then gradually exposes the model to larger blocks (B=8, 16, 32) to build coarse-grained parallel decoding capability.</p>

<div class="vp">
